In a public-repository model, doesn't there still have to be someone 
who says yes or no to individual proposed changes and patches?  What 
stops someone from uploading a "game balance fix" that puts a wand of
wishing with 12 charges in the starting room  ('Coz, obviously, the 
game is impossible without at least that, right)?   Or a "save and 
restore fix" that "modernizes" the game save and restore capabilities 
to allow players to save at any point and restore from any save ('Coz, 
duh, that's what save and restore does, why hadn't you guys fixed this
obvious bug already)?

There's a difference between open development and *wide* open 
development, and it requires rules and politics.  And not every bad 
idea will be as obviously bad as the two I mentioned above.  There 
are points where experienced devs who understand the game can 
disagree with good arguments on both (or all) sides, and some kind 
of procedure has to exist for the opinions of one (or some) to be 
picked over the opinions of others.  


